.TH OFTC 1 "October 15, 2026" "OFTC 1.0.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
oftc \- standalone macOS FortiGate IPsec VPN client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B oftc setup
.br
.B oftc setup
\fB\-\-import\fR \fINAME\fR
.br
.B oftc setup
\fB\-\-gateway\fR \fIHOST\fR \fB\-\-saml\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR [\fB\-\-tcp\fR] [\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR]
.br
.B oftc setup
\fB\-\-from\fR \fIFILE\fR
.br
.B oftc setup
\fB\-\-from\-development\fR
.br
.B oftc profiles
.br
.B oftc export
[\fIPROFILE\fR...] [\fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR]
.br
.B oftc connect
[\fIPROFILE\fR...]
.br
.B oftc disconnect
[\fIPROFILE\fR...]
.br
.B oftc remove
\fIPROFILE\fR...
.br
.B oftc status
[\fIPROFILE\fR...]
.br
.B oftc autostart
\fIPROFILE\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR
.br
.B oftc uninstall
[\fB\-\-erase\-profile\fR]
.br
.B oftc help
.SH DESCRIPTION
OFTC uses the signed macOS app to own the VPN configuration, Keychain PSK,
system extension, SAML sign-in, and supervised reconnection.
The package installs the app and this command at their standard paths.
Every configured VPN is a profile with its own macOS VPN entry, Keychain PSK,
supervisor and connect-at-login setting. Profiles are peers and may be
connected at the same time. A
.I PROFILE
argument is a profile id or its name; commands that take one act on every
profile when none is named.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Add a profile, activate OFTC's system extension, and add the profile's macOS
VPN configuration. It does not connect. Run it again to add another VPN. With no
options, ask which local settings to use; if profiles already exist and
standard input is not a terminal, resume setup for all of them. The OFTC development profile and FortiClient
profiles, when present, provide editable gateway and SAML port defaults. A
matching FortiClient credential is tried before asking for a PSK in the
terminal.
.TP
.BI "setup \-\-import " NAME
Import a local FortiClient profile and its PSK as a new profile named after it.
A gateway that already has a profile is refused. If the saved PSK is unavailable, use interactive
.B setup
to keep its connection settings and enter the PSK at the terminal prompt.
.TP
.BI "setup \-\-gateway " "HOST \-\-saml\-port PORT"
Save a new OFTC profile and prompt for its PSK in the terminal. UDP is the
default outer transport. Add
.B \-\-tcp
to select TCP.
.TP
.BI "setup \-\-from " FILE
Add the profiles in a file made by
.BR "oftc export" ,
then activate the system extension and add their macOS VPN configurations.
Use
.B \-
to read standard input. A gateway that already has a profile is skipped, so
importing never changes an existing profile or its PSK. The file carries each
profile's PSK, so no PSK prompt follows.
.TP
.B setup \-\-from\-development
Copy the nonsecret OFTC development profile and prompt for its PSK under the
release app's Keychain identity if no matching saved FortiClient PSK is
available. This is only for migration from the OFTC
development build. Stop the old VPN and its login agent first.
.TP
.B profiles
List configured profiles.
.TP
.B export
Write the named profiles, or all, with their PSKs, as one file for a colleague
to import with
.BR "oftc setup \-\-from" .
Without
.B \-o
the file goes to standard output; with it, the file is created with owner-only
permissions and an existing file is never overwritten. The PSK is not the
sign-in secret (SAML is), but the file is configuration for a private gateway,
so share it only with the people who need it.
.TP
.B connect
Start a supervised VPN connection for each named profile, or all profiles.
Supervisors run independently and log to
.IR ~/Library/Logs/OFTC/ID.log .
Browser sign-ins happen one at a time. A legacy per-user agent from an earlier
OFTC version is removed during the first connection. Recoverable connection
failures are retried by each supervisor.
.TP
.B disconnect
Stop the named profiles, or all. A deliberate disconnect stays down until the
next
.BR "oftc connect" .
It does not change a profile's connect-at-login setting.
.TP
.B remove
Delete the named profiles: stop each, then remove its macOS VPN entry, Keychain
PSK, settings, connect-at-login choice and saved gateway certificate. Other
profiles keep running. At least one profile must be named, and it cannot be
undone; use
.B oftc export
first to keep a copy. To add one again, import a
.B .oftc
file or run
.BR "oftc setup" .
.TP
.BI "autostart " "PROFILE " on|off
Connect that profile when you log in. The bundled login agent starts one
supervisor for every profile with this setting. New profiles start off.
.TP
.B status
Show each profile's VPN state, nonsecret settings and CHILD SA rekey counts
while connected, then login agent state and connect-at-login settings.
.TP
.B uninstall
Disconnect OFTC, close the menu app, unregister both login services, remove any legacy agent and VPN configuration,
deactivate its system extension, and invoke the package removal helper with
administrator access. Profile, Keychain PSK, and logs are retained by default.
The helper refuses to remove the app while the extension remains active.
.TP
.B uninstall \-\-erase\-profile
Also erase every OFTC profile and its exact Keychain PSK after disconnecting.
.TP
.B help
Show a short command summary.
.SH DEVELOPMENT OPTION
.TP
.BI "\-\-app " /absolute/path/OFTC.app
Prefix
.BR setup ,
.BR connect ,
.BR disconnect ,
or
.B status
to target a signed development build. Its bundled login agent is registered
from that app. Do not register two OFTC apps with the same bundle identifier.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /Applications/OFTC.app
Installed app and system extension.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/oftc
Command-line tool.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Logs/OFTC/ID.log
Per-profile supervised connection log.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/com.chris.openforticlient.oftc/profiles/ID/
Per-profile nonsecret settings and supervisor state.
.SH SEE ALSO
.B systemextensionsctl(8)
